            <div xml:id="d59">

                <pb facs="0189" n="90" sameAs="frus:frus1936v02/pg90"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.5131/1437C: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Straus</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-03-31T18:00:00-05:00">March 31, 1936—6 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>108. For your information only. The French Ambassador called yesterday and I told
                    him that we could not accept a one-sided trade agreement as the entire trade
                    agreements program would be discredited if any one of the agreements should be
                    taken off the solid basis of equality. I emphasized that the big objective was
                    to continue to advance this broad program for international business recovery. I
                    concluded by saying I felt that the French Government had not been <pb
                        facs="0190" n="91" xml:id="pg91"/> as mindful of these broad objectives as
                    it should and that it should assume a more liberal attitude with respect to
                    binding quotas and to granting requested concessions in view of these objectives
                    and of the substantial concessions we are prepared to make.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
